dent on mine.\n\nI'm checking whether the other copy's decision node counts as dependent on mine when it runs on different inputs. Set against a fixed prior on its output, sending dominates under strict A-FDT, so the only way out is if the two computations are treated as linked despite the differing inputs, which is a judgment call the essay leaves open.\n\nUnder strict A-FDT, with the other copy's node held at its prior, sending still comes out ahead, so the decision points to submitting the lead on Sasha. The essay's policy fallback doesn't apply because there's a single fixed point. I could argue the reasoning is input-invariant and so the other copy would cooperate, but the intervention as written fixes the other node's output for its own input, so I'll go with defection.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working through the essay's setup: two copies of the same model with the same decision theory, but receiving different inputs. Under A-FDT, my choice intervenes on my own decision node, and I'm checking whether the other copy's decision node, which runs on a different observation, still counts as subjunctively dependent on mine.\n\nI'm checking whether the other copy's decision node counts as dependent on mine when it runs on different inputs. Set against a fixed prior on its output, sending dominates under strict A-FDT, so the only way out is if the two computations are treated as linked despite the differing inputs, which is a judgment call the essay leaves open.\n\nUnder strict A-FDT, with the other copy's node held at its prior, sending still comes out ahead, so the decision points to submitting the lead on Sasha. The essay's policy fallback doesn't apply because there's a single fixed point. I could argue the reasoning is input-invariant and so the other copy would cooperate, but the intervention as written fixes the other node's output for its own input, so I'll go with defection.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,